<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>







<!-- Generated by Pandoc using pretext.lua -->
<section xml:id="sec-acidsbases6" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
	<title>Neutralization reactions for monoprotic acids and bases</title>

	<p>
		The other class of reaction that we've seen before in this chapter is a
		neutralization reaction.
		Here we'll cover it in more detail.
		If the neutralization reaction is between a strong acid and strong base, then
		the products will be water and a salt as shown in the equations below:
	</p>

	<p>
		Chemical equation:
	</p>
	<me>
		HCl\,\text{(aq)} + NaOH\,\text{(aq)} \rightarrow H_2O\,\text{(l)} + NaCl\,\text{(aq)}
	</me>

	<p>
		Ionic equation:
	</p>
	<me>
		H^+\,\text{(aq)} + Cl^-\,\text{(aq)} + Na^+\,\text{(aq)} + OH^-\,\text{(aq)} \rightarrow H_2O\,\text{(l)} + Na^+\,\text{(aq)} + Cl^-\,\text{(aq)}
	</me>

	<p>
		Net ionic equation:
	</p>
	<me>
		H^+\,\text{(aq)} + OH^-\,\text{(aq)} \rightarrow H_2O\,\text{(l)}
	</me>

	<p>
		Any neutralization reaction involving a strong acid reacting with a strong
		base will have an ionic equation of:
	</p>
	<me>
		H^+\,\text{(aq)} + OH^-\,\text{(aq)} \rightarrow H_2O\,\text{(l)}
	</me>

	<p>
		Steps to writing a neutralization reaction:
	</p>

	<p>
		<ol>
			<li>
				<p>
					Identify the acid and the base
				</p>

				<ol>
					<li>
						<p>
							Strong acids will always be the acid and strong bases will always be the
							base
						</p>
					</li>

					<li>
						<p>
							In reactions between a weak acid and a weak base, the acid and base will have to be specified
						</p>
					</li>
				</ol>
			</li>
			<li>
				<p>
							Break the reactants into their ions
				</p>

					<ol>
						<li>
							<p>
									Separate strong acids into <m>H^+</m> and the accompanying anion	
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
										Separate strong bases into the cation and <m>OH^-</m>
							</p>
						</li>
					
								
						<li>
							<p>
									Covalent compounds do not separate into ions
							</p>
								<ol>
									<li>
										<p>
												Example: <m>NH_3</m> is a covalent compound and thus won't break apart into ions.There is no hydroxide it can lose.
										</p>
									</li>
								</ol>
						
								
						</li>

						<li>
							<p>
									If the acid is a polyatomic ion (ie. 		one of the polyatomic ions with “hydrogen” in the name) then break off the <m>H^+</m> ion
							</p>
								<ol>
									<li>
										<p>
											Example: <m>HPO_4^{2-}</m> breaks into <m>H^+</m> and <m>PO_4^{3-}</m> if it is acting as an acid
										</p>
									</li>
								</ol>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								If a polyatomic ion is a base, don't do anything to it yet.
							</p>
						</li>
					
				</ol>
			</li>
			<li>
				<p>
					React the acid and the base
				</p>
					<ol>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>H^+</m> and <m>OH^-</m> will combine together (if present) to form water
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								If hydroxide isn't present, transfer the <m>H^+</m> to whatever base is in the reaction
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						Look at what ions you have left over (and what new ion you may have formed)
					</p>
					<ol>
						<li>
							<p>
								If you're being asked for an ionic equation, write these ions in the products
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								If you're being asked for
		a net ionic equation, delete any ions that didn't change in the reaction
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								If you're being asked for
		the chemical equation, combine the ions together to form ionic compounds
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</li>
		</ol>
	
	</p>

	<p>
		To write a neutralization reaction, like we just did above, we first split
		acid and base into their ions.
		Then we allowed the acid (<m>H^+</m>) to react with the base (<m>OH^-</m>) to
		form water.
		This results in ions being left over (the spectator ions).
		Those ions can then be...
	</p>

	<p>
		<ol>
			<li>
				<p>
					Combined together to form ionic compounds, giving us the chemical equation,
				</p>
			</li>
			<li>
				<p>
					Left alone as separate ions, forming the ionic equation, or
				</p>
			</li>
			<li>
				<p>
					Deleted in both the reactants and products to form the net ionic equation.
				</p>
			</li>
		</ol>
		
	</p>

	<p>
		Neutralization reactions can follow slightly different patterns when the base
		is NOT hydroxide.
		For instance, if the base is ammonia (<m>NH_3</m> ), then we need to first
		recognize that this is a covalent compound, not an ionic compound.
		Thus it won't split into ions in the first step.
		See the example below:
	</p>

	<p>
		If we react <m>NH_3\,\text{(aq)}</m> with <m>HCl\,\text{(aq)}</m>, after step
		one, we’d have <m>NH_3\,\text{(aq)}</m> + <m>H^+\,\text{(aq)}</m> +
		<m>Cl^-\,\text{(aq)}</m>.
		At this point the acidic <m>H^+</m> ion will react with the basic <m>NH_3</m>
		to form <m>NH_4^+</m>.
		The product would then contain <m>NH_4^+\,\text{(aq)}</m> and the left over
		<m>Cl^-\,\text{(aq)}</m>.
		We can then write the equation in the following ways:
	</p>

	<p>
		Ionic equation:
	</p>
	<me>
		NH_3\,\text{(aq)} + H^+\,\text{(aq)} + Cl^-\,\text{(aq)} \rightarrow NH_4^+\,\text{(aq)} + Cl^-\,\text{(aq)}
	</me>

	<p>
		Net ionic equation (get rid of the chloride spectator ion):
	</p>
	<me>
		NH_3\,\text{(aq)} + H^+\,\text{(aq)} \rightarrow NH_4^+\,\text{(aq)}
	</me>

	<p>
		Chemical equation (combine the ions in the products together to make a
		compound):
	</p>
	<me>
		NH_3\,\text{(aq)} + HCl\,\text{(aq)} \rightarrow NH_4Cl\,\text{(aq)}
	</me>

	<p>
		What if the base is a polyatomic ion, like hydrogen carbonate.
		Then, just like ammonia, it will not dissociate (because there is no hydroxide
		that it can lose).
		It will instead combine with the hydrogen ion to form a new substance.
		See the example below:
	</p>

	<p>
		If we react <m>NaHSO_4\,\text{(aq)}</m>with <m>HCl\,\text{(aq)}</m>, after
		step one, we’d have <m>Na^+\,\text{(aq)}</m> + <m>H_2PO_4^-\,\text{(aq)}</m> +
		<m>H^+\,\text{(aq)}</m> + <m>Cl^-\,\text{(aq)}</m>.
		In step 2, the <m>HCl</m> is identified as the acid since it has an <m>H^+</m>
		ion; therefore the sodium dihydrogen phosphate must be the base.
		At this point the acidic <m>H^+</m> ion will react with the basic
		<m>H_2PO_4^-</m> to form <m>H_3PO_4</m>.
		This leaves a left over <m>Na^+\,\text{(aq)}</m> + <m>Cl^-\,\text{(aq)}</m>.
		We can then write the equation in the following ways:
	</p>

	<p>
		Ionic equation:
	</p>
	<me>
		Na^+\,\text{(aq)} + H_2PO_4^-\,\text{(aq)} + H^+\,\text{(aq)} + Cl^-\,\text{(aq)} \rightarrow H_3PO_4\,\text{(aq)} + Na^+\,\text{(aq)} + Cl^-\,\text{(aq)}
	</me>

	<p>
		Net ionic equation (get rid of the spectator ions):
	</p>
	<me>
		H_2PO_4^-\,\text{(aq)} + H^+\,\text{(aq)} \rightarrow H_3PO_4\,\text{(aq)}
	</me>

	<p>
		Chemical equation (combine the ions in the products together to make a
		compound):
	</p>
	<me>
		NaH_2PO_4\,\text{(aq)} + HCl\,\text{(aq)} \rightarrow H_3PO_4\,\text{(aq)} + NaCl\,\text{(aq)}
	</me>

	<p>
		As a final nuance, we can also have cases where polyatomic ions can act as
		acids.
		In those cases, the polyatomic ion will have “hydrogen” as a part of its name.
		This hydrogen will be lost to the base.
		Therefore, this polyatomic ion must be broken apart by losing its <m>H^+</m>.
		See the example below:
	</p>

	<p>
		Write a chemical equation, ionic equation, and net ionic equation for the
		reaction between sodium hydrogen phosphate and potassium hydroxide.
	</p>

	<p>
		1.
		Start by writing the formulas of the compounds and then breaking them apart
		into their ions Sodium hydrogen phosphate: <m>2Na^+\,\text{(aq)}</m> and
		<m>HPO_4^{2-}\,\text{(aq)}</m> Potassium hydroxide: <m>K^+\,\text{(aq)}</m>
		and <m>OH^-\,\text{(aq)}</m> 2.
		The base is the potassium hydroxide, thus the hydrogen phosphate is acting as
		an acid.
		It must be further broken apart into <m>H^+</m> and <m>PO_4^{3-}</m>.
		3.
		React the acid and the base.
		In this case we have <m>H^+</m> and <m>OH^-</m> that will combine to form
		water.
		This leaves behind <m>2Na^+</m>, <m>K^+</m>, and <m>PO_4^{3-}</m>.
		4.
		We can now write the ionic equation,
		<m>K^+\,\text{(aq)} + OH^-\,\text{(aq)} + 2Na^+\,\text{(aq)} + H^+\,\text{(aq)} + PO_4^{3-}\,\text{(aq)} \rightarrow H_2O\,\text{(l)} + 2Na^+\,\text{(aq)} + K^+\,\text{(aq)} + PO_4^{3-}\,\text{(aq)}</m>
		net ionic equation,
	</p>

	<p>
		<m>OH^-\,\text{(aq)} + H^+\,\text{(aq)} \rightarrow H_2O\,\text{(l)}</m> and
		chemical equation
		<m>KOH\,\text{(aq)} + Na_2HPO_4\,\text{(aq)} \rightarrow H_2O\,\text{(l)} + Na_2KPO_4\,\text{(aq)}</m>
	</p>

	<p>
		(note the strange ionic compound in the product with two different
		cations...that’s okay!...also their order in the formula doesn’t matter)
	</p>

	<exercises>
		<title>Exercise 1</title>

		<exercisegroup>
			<introduction>
				<p>
					Write an ionic equation, net ionic equation, and chemical equation for the
					following reactions:
				</p>
			</introduction>

			<exercise>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Potassium hydroxide and hydrobromic acid
					</p>
				</statement>
			</exercise>

			<exercise>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Nitric acid and sodium hydroxide
					</p>
				</statement>
			</exercise>

			<exercise>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Ammonia and hydroiodic acid
					</p>
				</statement>
			</exercise>

			<exercise>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Potassium hydroxide and potassium hydrogen carbonate
					</p>
				</statement>
			</exercise>

			<exercise>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Nitric acid and sodium hydrogen carbonate
					</p>
				</statement>
			</exercise>

			<exercise>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Ammonia and sodium hydrogen carbonate
					</p>
				</statement>
			</exercise>
		</exercisegroup>
	</exercises>
</section>